{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The causal mechanism is mis-specified. SRC_001 proposes invariance-to-rephrasing as the operational mark of a genuine reliability gain. But for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the causal mechanism that makes a checklist reliable is precisely that it pins down the decision-relevant features into an explicit, low-ambiguity procedure. Rephrasing the checklist introduces ambiguity, so a performance drop under rephrasing is the expected consequence of losing the procedural scaffold, not evidence that panelists were 'merely complying.' Conversely, a panel that stays high under rephrasing might simply be ignoring the checklist and using prior belief. Thus the rephrasing test does not validly distinguish genuine reliability from compliance; it conflates scaffold-fidelity with understanding.","counterexample":[{"task":"grading a structured rubric for loan-default prediction","panel_v1":"checklist states: if debt-to-income > 0.43, mark reject","panel_v2":"rephrased checklist states: if the borrower's obligations substantially exceed a prudent threshold, mark reject","observation":"panel performance drops under panel_v2 because the rephrased rule is ambiguous and under-specified, yet the original panel_v1 performance was a genuine reliability gain produced by the checklist's explicit threshold — not mere compliance"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The proposed causal mechanism is empirically unidentifiable within a single evaluation. SRC_002 demands a 'mediator model of the outcome' revealing a significant understanding residual after controlling for checklist score, and stipulates that the gain is genuine iff this residual is significant. But in one evaluation, 'checklist score' (the panel's application of the rule) and 'understanding residual' (panel-specific insight beyond the rule) are not separable mediators without exogenous variation in the checklist itself. Absent such variation, any residual is just the unexplained component of outcome variance, not a causally-identified panel-understanding pathway. The target therefore fails to provide a distinguishable mechanism, since the 'residual' could equally be noise, task-features the checklist omitted, or shared panel prior — none of which establish the claimed causal mechanism of distributed collective intelligence.","counterexample":[{"within_single_evaluation":"with only one checklist version and one outcome per item, the decomposition of outcome into checklist-driven vs. residual-understanding components is not point-identified","confound":"a large positive residual may reflect an unmodeled outcome feature (e.g., base rate) rather than panel insight, and the single-evaluation design cannot distinguish these"}]}]}